WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:10.130
In case you missed it, I'm just back from
a whopping 10 day social media and news hiatus.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:15.610
Yes, believe it or not it's true, I didn't
look at my Twitter feed or follow current

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.279
events for 10 whole days.

00:00:18.279 --> 00:00:23.290
Sadly, going offline for more than a couple
hours seems like a super human feat these

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:24.900
days, doesn't it?

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.500
Just having one single day without looking
at your phone, without reading the news and

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.610
without reacting to someone else's opinion,
most likely someone else you've never even

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:36.160
met, is becoming increasingly rare.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:41.230
This rarity, the ability to live life without
being constantly connected, is going to become

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:46.570
more and more important as our physical world
continues to collide with the digital world.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:51.900
We're so connected these days that we can't
stop the onslaught of information for any

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:57.230
amount of time that would allow us to figure
out what's important and what is irrelevant.

00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:01.630
Or perhaps more importantly, what's fact and
what's fiction.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:06.450
It's very obvious to me that occasionally
pausing this endless stream of noise is going

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:11.240
to be one of the biggest challenges that faces
humanity in the next decade.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.841
This challenge will be vital not only for
millennials, but for old time gen Xers like

00:01:15.841 --> 00:01:19.359
me as well as the baby boomers who came before
us.

00:01:19.359 --> 00:01:24.100
As for the kids growing up with an iPad in
the crib, well good luck with all of that.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:28.369
By doing pretty much nothing on my vacation
besides laying at the beach, going fishing

00:01:28.369 --> 00:01:33.850
and taking bike rides, I was able to reset
a little of this mental clutter.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:38.009
This reset was kind of tough for the first
couple days, even though I tested my off the

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:40.340
grid skills less than a year ago.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:46.179
Last time, I literally locked my phone in
a safe for a week to disconnect, but this

00:01:46.179 --> 00:01:50.570
time I just put my phone on a shelf in the
closet in a shoe box under a couple other

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:51.570
boxes.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.950
I guess that's some sort of progress right
there.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:57.709
The first couple days I had the knee jerk
reaction to go to my pocket and look at my

00:01:57.709 --> 00:02:00.850
phone for no reason at all on more than one
occasion.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:05.990
Eventually that wore off and I found a strange
sense of freedom in not being online.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:09.759
by the final days of my vacation part of me
didn't want to come back online at all.

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:14.850
Apparently while I was off the grid, the world
didn't stop without me.

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:20.270
From the Milo scandal, to Trump banning CNN
from the White House press briefings, to the

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:24.020
Oscars best picture screw up, the world kept
on spinning along.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:27.630
If you missed it, I did a live stream this
past Monday with my thoughts on some of the

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:31.950
craziness I missed, we'll post the link to
that right down below.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:35.620
Putting aside these specific news stories
and current events, I noticed something else

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.530
as I disconnected from the Matrix.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:42.650
I actually felt calmer, more present, and
generally happier by being offline.

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:47.850
Now maybe this had something to do with the
beach and the margaritas, but it wasn't just

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:48.850
that.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:53.820
I don't think our brains are wired to be taking
in endless amounts of information all the

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:54.840
time.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:00.200
200 years ago to get a message from London
to New York you had to write a letter, get

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:04.350
it on a boat, wait a couple weeks for it to
get there, and then wait another couple weeks

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.750
for the response to get back across the pond
to you.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:13.320
100 years ago our fastest communication was
a relatively new invention known as the telephone

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:18.820
which now seems totally archaic. 50 years
ago we only had a handful of television channels

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:23.540
and 20 years ago cell phones were so big they
made Zach Morris' phone on Saved by the Bell

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:24.880
look small.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:28.890
Soon enough you'll be able to stream live
video aboard a supersonic plane that will

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:32.730
take you from New York to London in about
20 minutes.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:38.260
The point is, the world is now speeding up
at an incredible pace, and our own evolution,

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:43.360
the ability for our minds to evolve along
with the technology isn't changing at the

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:44.530
same pace.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:48.770
I believe this goes a long way to explaining
why people are constantly outraged and always

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:51.680
ready to fight with random strangers online.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.600
Our Connectivity is evolving faster than our
conscience.

00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:02.841
There's no way for us to stop this scientific
progress, but we can certainly try to deal

00:04:02.841 --> 00:04:04.700
with its side effects more effectively.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:11.319
And by the way, you've all heard me say how
incredible this online connection can be.

00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:17.509
This is the irony of our current situation,
we all have an awesome power at our fingertips,

00:04:17.509 --> 00:04:20.299
but it's up to us what to do with it.

00:04:20.299 --> 00:04:24.660
As Uncle Ben said to Peter Parker, "with great
power comes great responsibility."

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:31.550
Talking, or more appropriately, fighting about
politics endlessly isn't good for our brains

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:32.740
or for our society.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:39.700
One of the reasons I'm for small government
is because a good government is one that you

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.389
don't have to think about too often.

00:04:42.389 --> 00:04:45.650
And if you don't have to think about it too
often, you don't have to tweet about it every

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:50.290
five seconds or write endless Facebook diatribes
about it either.

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:55.960
If the government doesn't have too much control
over your life, you don't have to fixate over

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:59.860
every move it makes or every policy it institutes.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:03.000
This is always an incredible irony of the
progressive ideology.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.960
They constantly complain about how the government
operates, but their solution is always to

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:08.860
make the government bigger.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:14.170
If only their guys were in charge then we'd
have some sort of magical utopia.

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:18.170
This isn't only childish thinking, it's also
just against human nature.

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:23.410
The only way to rear in power is to rear it
in regardless of who's in charge.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:27.860
The feeling that we all have to comment on
every news story, to fight over every political

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:33.520
point and to debate every little issue is
exactly why a smaller government would be

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:35.180
better these days.

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:36.180
Don't like Trump?

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:40.840
The answer isn't to try to upend democracy,
the answer is to reign in the power of government.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:46.350
If we could only live and let live as our
founders intended, we would spend less time

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:50.570
worrying, fighting and distracting ourselves
from our own lives.

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:54.690
So to wrap this up I'm deleting this channel,
closing my Twitter account and shutting down

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:55.690
my Facebook.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:59.800
We've had a good run with The Rubin Report,
but nothing lasts forever.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:06.550
Just kidding, but I am going to make a renewed
commitment not add meaningless noise to your

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:08.430
already hectic life.

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:12.419
Now share this video with everyone you know
or the world as you know it will come to a

00:06:12.419 --> 00:06:12.909
horrible end.

